WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:01.810
Where are you going?

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:03.860
This outfit with this hair?

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:07.120
Hello, bye bye, I am so at the mall.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:11.950
Between 1970 and 2017, the number of American
malls quadrupled.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:16.450
Population didn’t even double in the same
time frame.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.470
Malls were opening really, really fast.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.480
That growth slowed down.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.640
About a quarter of the malls could close in
the next 5 years.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:29.539
Some people call this the death of the mall
— and that’s kind of true.

00:00:29.539 --> 00:00:33.750
Here’s why that matters.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:41.140
Our lives are lived in 1 of 3 places:

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.739
The first is the home.

00:00:42.739 --> 00:00:45.260
The second is where you work.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:51.760
And the ‘third place’... well that’s
anywhere outside of those two.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.770
There are 8 qualities that make for great
third places:

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:59.960
It sounds kind of academic, but trust me you
know it when you see them:

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.290
The best “third places” invite conversation

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.820
They’re close to home.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:09.070
And lots of time can be spent without spending
much money

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:14.640
These are places where people can exchange
ideas, have a good time, and build relationships.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.590
In the 1950s, America had a problem:

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:24.850
Their brand new suburbs lacked quality third
places.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:26.720
This guy wanted to solve that problem.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.920
So he designed the first enclosed shopping
mall.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.270
This is Southdale Center.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.270
The world's most fabulous shopping center...

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:38.810
The format caught on like wildfire, fueled
by tax incentives for new developments —&nbsp;that

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:39.939
included malls.

00:01:39.939 --> 00:01:45.600
Soon, they were being built by the dozen.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.660
Over the next 50 years there were over 1000
enclosed shopping malls scattered across America.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:56.170
Along the way, they became the hangout for
American youth.

00:01:56.170 --> 00:02:03.060
The thing about that is, even Victor Gruen
admitted his design was a monumental failure.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.720
Because when you look at the qualities of
great third places… malls deliver on some,

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:08.879
but not all.

00:02:08.879 --> 00:02:12.620
They’re safe, and well lit

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.540
No one has to play host at the mall

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.709
And they’re accessible no matter the weather.

00:02:19.709 --> 00:02:24.749
But on the flip side, you can’t really walk
to the mall — you need a car.

00:02:24.749 --> 00:02:28.159
And once you’re there, conversation should
be the main activity.

00:02:28.159 --> 00:02:32.209
In a mall, that’s shopping.

00:02:32.209 --> 00:02:35.290
Third places should make everyone feel equal.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:38.430
The mall, by contrast, is more fun for the
rich.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:44.099
Now contrast that with a different take on
the third place — the National Mall in Washington

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:45.099
DC.

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:48.040
It’s open for long hours

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.040
The mood is playful

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.749
It’s free.

00:02:51.749 --> 00:02:56.059
You can visit with a short walk, or by public
transportation.

00:02:56.059 --> 00:02:58.090
With indoor and outdoor public spaces

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:05.299
This adds up to an environment that fosters...
community.

00:03:05.299 --> 00:03:13.879
When you look at these two side by side, which
would you choose?

00:03:13.879 --> 00:03:18.090
while some malls are being repurposed, others
are just getting torn down...

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:24.650
This is in part because of the recession in
2008; people had less money to spend.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:31.140
While more than 90% of purchases in the US
are still made in physicals store… more

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.019
people are getting their goods online.

00:03:33.019 --> 00:03:37.809
And we aren’t just shopping online.

00:03:37.809 --> 00:03:42.239
Our relationships and connections are going
digital as well.

00:03:42.239 --> 00:03:47.359
More than 3 in 4 people in the US has a smartphone.

00:03:47.359 --> 00:03:52.239
When social media is used to actively connect
with others, it can be like a digital “third

00:03:52.239 --> 00:03:53.239
place.”

00:03:53.239 --> 00:03:58.249
But studies like this one show that some digital
communication can affect your mental or even

00:03:58.249 --> 00:04:02.559
physical health.

00:04:02.559 --> 00:04:05.599
The mall occupies a unique space in America’s
conscience.

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:10.290
And that’s largely because we have an innate
need for face-to-face contact.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:15.889
And in some areas, the mall was that place.

00:04:15.889 --> 00:04:21.590
But as more malls shut down so too are other
third places, and the replacements often have

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:23.700
little stake in the local community.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:31.520
But there is a better way — consider the
public beaches of downtown Detroit, or the

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.349
redevelopment of Bryant Park in New York City.

00:04:34.349 --> 00:04:38.199
Or, you could just support a locally-owned
coffee shop.

00:04:38.199 --> 00:04:42.840
So the decline of the mall doesn’t need
to be the death of the third place –it’s

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.869
up to us to decide where those spaces will
be.

